{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-79818" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot y={e}^{x},\\,x>0\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-79818" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)={e}^{x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)=x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot y) & =x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y & =x{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx) & =x{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-79818" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0f11112cb65b3e750933ec31424c8747ef9562283a56dd3eadf84f0b9e159d1c"} {"question" "q-79818" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-79818" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot {e}^{x}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx)=x{e}^{x}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-x})={e}^{-x}{e}^{x}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}{e}^{x}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2})={x}^{2}{e}^{x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-79818" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx)=x{e}^{x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-79819" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\nx\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=1\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-79819" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation in standard form. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}\\cdot y=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{2}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}\\cdot y) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2}) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-79819" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0f11112cb65b3e750933ec31424c8747ef9562283a56dd3eadf84f0b9e159d1c"} {"question" "q-79819" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-79819" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot 2)=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2})={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-79819" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2})={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-335278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{1}{2}y={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-335278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{3}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-1/2)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={e}^{-x/2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x/2}\\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{1}{2}y) & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{-x/2}\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\cdot y & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{-x/2}) & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-335278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0f11112cb65b3e750933ec31424c8747ef9562283a56dd3eadf84f0b9e159d1c"} {"question" "q-335278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-335278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}))=-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {x}^{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{2}))=-\\frac{x}{2}\\cdot {x}^{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot 2)=2{x}^{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{-x/2})={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{x/2})={e}^{x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-335278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{-x/2})={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-17539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+xy={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-17539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation in standard form. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)=x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot y) & =x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}+y & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(yx) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-17539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0f11112cb65b3e750933ec31424c8747ef9562283a56dd3eadf84f0b9e159d1c"} {"question" "q-17539" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-17539" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{-x})={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{x}^{2})={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-17539" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-17533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\nx\\frac{dy}{dx}+xy={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-17533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation in standard form. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+y=x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=1$ and $Q(x)=x.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 1\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={e}^{x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx}+y) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot x \\\\ {e}^{x}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{e}^{x}y & ={e}^{x}\\cdot x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{x}) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot x\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-17533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0f11112cb65b3e750933ec31424c8747ef9562283a56dd3eadf84f0b9e159d1c"} {"question" "q-17533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-17533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot 2)=2x"] ["math" "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot 2x)=2x\\cdot x"] ["math" "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx)=x\\cdot x"] ["math" "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{2x})={e}^{2x}\\cdot x"] ["math" "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}\\cdot x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-17533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}\\cdot x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-335412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}},\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-335412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{3}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={x}^{3}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y) & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot y & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(y{x}^{3})\\,dx & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & ={e}^{x}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+C}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-335412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0f11112cb65b3e750933ec31424c8747ef9562283a56dd3eadf84f0b9e159d1c"} {"question" "q-335412" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course