043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "q-335811" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/state.json" "file_sha256" "262ce129f7f1f65c402783c2d66bedb70122542fa8239199acd5449d117bec1a"} {"question" "e-21741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 2y = 2e^{-2x}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x) \\right) = I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/example-21741.json" "file_sha256" "dd1342bf4c9fc5408154ed35f946fedab059885f2ff411054723a2e2f587074f"} {"question" "e-21741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = 2$ and $Q(x) = 2e^{-2x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = e^{2x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{2x}\\cdot y & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x)\\right){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{2x}) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/example-21741.json" "file_sha256" "dd1342bf4c9fc5408154ed35f946fedab059885f2ff411054723a2e2f587074f"} {"question" "e-21742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution of the following differential equation using the method of integrating factors.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac {3} {x} y = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}, \\qquad x \\gt 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/example-21742.json" "file_sha256" "7355f71d41485fce56c0c682273d58106f681338e6d68f18625b803a1d435f8f"} {"question" "e-21742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = \\dfrac{3}{x}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = x^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y) & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot y & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3}) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3})\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this integral, we integrate by parts. \n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm d x = uv - \\int u' v\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nLet $u = x, u' = 1, v' = \\cos x,$ and $v = \\sin x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/example-21742.json" "file_sha256" "7355f71d41485fce56c0c682273d58106f681338e6d68f18625b803a1d435f8f"} {"question" "e-21743" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the following initial value problem using the method of integrating factors.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 3x^2y = 9x^2, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/example-21743.json" "file_sha256" "6f63b87193633123b6d76ee7b0e99bd63ef5a8fb93d4888f11a1c3a43d656934"} {"question" "e-21743" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = 3x^2$ and $Q(x) = 9x^2.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x) = e^{x^3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}y) & ={e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot 9{x}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot y & =9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{3}}) & =9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x,$ making use of the substitution $u = x^3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{3}})\\text{d}x & =\\int 9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =\\int 3{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =3{e}^{u}+C \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =3{e}^{{x}^{3}}+C \\\\ y & =3+C{e}^{-{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0) = 1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =3+C{e}^{-{0}^{3}} \\\\ 1 & =3+C \\\\ C & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 3 - 2e^{-x^3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043121/example-21743.json" "file_sha256" "6f63b87193633123b6d76ee7b0e99bd63ef5a8fb93d4888f11a1c3a43d656934"}] "usage" {"q-335810" [] "e-21741" [{"kind" "presentations" "entity" 17592186283546}] "e-21742" [{"kind" "presentations" "entity" 17592186283892}] "e-21743" []} "basis" 1602 "retractions" [[17592186088087 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n$x\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2y = 4x^{2}, y(1) = 2$\nSelect the correct options below. An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is $y(x) =$ {{field-2}}."] [17592186088087 :question/worked-solution "On the interval $x>0$ containing the initial point, divide by $x$ to obtain $y^{\\prime}+\\frac2x y=4x$. An integrating factor is $I(x)=x^2$. Then $(x^2y)^{\\prime}=4x^3$, so $x^2y=x^4+C$ and $y=x^2+C/x^2$. Since $y(1)=2$, $C=1$. Therefore $y=x^2+\\frac1{x^2}$ for $x>0$."] [17592186087387 :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)$:\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 2y = 2e^{-2x}$\nWrite the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x) \\right) = I(x)Q(x)$, where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor."] [17592186087387 :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x) = 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x) = 2e^{-2x}\n$$\n\nWe proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= e^{\\int P(x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{\\int 2dx} \\\\\n&= e^{2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by\n\n$$\nI(x) = e^{2x}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{2x} \\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2y) &= e^{2x} \\cdot 2e^{-2x} \\\\\ne^{2x} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} + 2e^{2x} \\cdot y &= e^{2x} \\cdot 2e^{-2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x)\\right){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{2x}) &= e^{2x} \\cdot 2e^{-2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087388 :question/problem "Find the general solution of the following differential equation using the method of integrating factors.\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac {3} {x} y = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}, \\qquad x \\gt 0$."] [17592186087388 :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x) = \\dfrac{3}{x}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x) = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}\n$$\n\nWe proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= e^{\\int P(x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{\\int (3/x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{3\\ln x} \\\\\n&= e^{\\ln (x^{3})} \\\\\n&= x^3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = x^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} \\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{3}{x}y) &= x^{3} \\cdot \\frac{\\cos x}{x^{2}} \\\\\nx^{3} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} + 3x^{2} \\cdot y &= x\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)\n$$\n\nand carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(yx^{3}) &= x\\cos x \\\\\n\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(yx^{3})dx &= \\int x\\cos xdx \\\\\nyx^{3} &= \\int x\\cos xdx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this integral, we integrate by parts.\n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm d x = uv - \\int u' v\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\nu = x, u' = 1, v' = \\cos x\n$$\n\nand $v = \\sin x$. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nyx^{3} &= \\int x\\cos xdx \\\\\nyx^{3} &= x\\sin x - \\int \\sin xdx \\\\\nyx^{3} &= x\\sin x + \\cos x + C \\\\\ny &= \\frac{x\\sin x+\\cos x}{x^3}+\\frac{C}{x^3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087389 :question/problem "Solve the following initial value problem using the method of integrating factors.\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 3x^2y = 9x^2, \\qquad y(0) = 1$"] [17592186087389 :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x) = 3x^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x) = 9x^2\n$$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= e^{\\int P(x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{\\int 3x^{2}dx} \\\\\n&= e^{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by\n\n$$\nI(x) = e^{x^3}\n$$\n\nyields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{x^{3}} \\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx} + 3x^{2}y) &= e^{x^{3}} \\cdot 9x^{2} \\\\\ne^{x^{3}} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} + 3x^{2}e^{x^{3}} \\cdot y &= 9x^{2}e^{x^{3}} \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x^{3}}) &= 9x^{2}e^{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$, making use of the substitution $u = x^3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x^{3}})dx &= \\int 9x^{2}e^{x^{3}}dx \\\\\nye^{x^{3}} &= \\int 3e^{u}du \\\\\nye^{x^{3}} &= 3e^{u} + C \\\\\nye^{x^{3}} &= 3e^{x^{3}} + C \\\\\ny &= 3 + Ce^{-x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0) = 1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(0) &= 3 + Ce^{-0^{3}} \\\\\n1 &= 3 + C \\\\\nC &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 3 - 2e^{-x^3}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186085450 17592186085451 17592186085452 17592186085453 17592186085455 17592186085456 17592186085457 17592186085458] "immutable_fields" [[17592186085449 :answer-field/correct] [17592186085449 :answer-field/id] [17592186085449 :answer-field/key] [17592186085449 :answer-field/type] [17592186085454 :answer-field/correct] [17592186085454 :answer-field/id] [17592186085454 :answer-field/key] [17592186085454 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
